        "sections": [
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>The <strong>product rule for exponents</strong> states that when we multiply two or more exponents that have the same base, we add the exponents:</p>",
            "readable_text": "The product rule for exponents states that when we multiply two or more exponents that have the same base, we add the exponents:",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: a^n\\cdot a^m=a^{n+m}]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: a^n\\cdot a^m=a^{n+m}]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>For example,</p>",
            "readable_text": "For example,",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: a^2\\cdot a^3 = a^{2+3} = a^5.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: a^2\\cdot a^3 = a^{2+3} = a^5.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>To see why the product rule is true, let&#x27;s take the above example and work it out again, the long way.</p>",
            "readable_text": "To see why the product rule is true, let's take the above example and work it out again, the long way.",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>For the two factors <span class=\"math\">[MATH: a^2]</span> and <span class=\"math\">[MATH: a^3]</span> we have <span class=\"math\">[MATH: a^2=a\\cdot a]</span> and <span class=\"math\">[MATH: a^3= a\\cdot a\\cdot a.]</span> If we now substitute these two expressions into <span class=\"math\">[MATH: a^2\\cdot a^3]</span>, we get the same result:\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\eqalign{\na^2\\cdot a^3&amp;=\\\n(a\\cdot a) \\cdot (a\\cdot a\\cdot a)&amp;=\\\na\\cdot a\\cdot a\\cdot a\\cdot a&amp;=\\\na^5\n}]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "For the two factors [MATH: a^2] and [MATH: a^3] we have [MATH: a^2=a\\cdot a] and [MATH: a^3= a\\cdot a\\cdot a.] If we now substitute these two expressions into [MATH: a^2\\cdot a^3], we get the same result:\n[MATH: \\eqalign{\na^2\\cdot a^3&=\\\n(a\\cdot a) \\cdot (a\\cdot a\\cdot a)&=\\\na\\cdot a\\cdot a\\cdot a\\cdot a&=\\\na^5\n}]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>The product rule is just a shortcut for the process above.</p>",
            "readable_text": "The product rule is just a shortcut for the process above.",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
        ],
        "images": [],
        "free_entry_blanks": [],
        "select_lists": [],
        "readable_text": "Content:\nThe product rule for exponents states that when we multiply two or more exponents that have the same base, we add the exponents:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: a^n\\cdot a^m=a^{n+m}]\n\nContent:\nFor example,\n\nContent:\n[MATH: a^2\\cdot a^3 = a^{2+3} = a^5.]\n\nContent:\nTo see why the product rule is true, let's take the above example and work it out again, the long way.\n\nContent:\nFor the two factors [MATH: a^2] and [MATH: a^3] we have [MATH: a^2=a\\cdot a] and [MATH: a^3= a\\cdot a\\cdot a.] If we now substitute these two expressions into [MATH: a^2\\cdot a^3], we get the same result:\n[MATH: \\eqalign{\na^2\\cdot a^3&=\\\n(a\\cdot a) \\cdot (a\\cdot a\\cdot a)&=\\\na\\cdot a\\cdot a\\cdot a\\cdot a&=\\\na^5\n}]\n\nContent:\nThe product rule is just a shortcut for the process above."
      },
