          {
            "section_type": "example_explanation",
            "normalized_html": "<p>Here we have two variables, <span class=\"math\">[MATH: x]</span> and <span class=\"math\">[MATH: y.]</span> We will start by grouping the two variables separately: \n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: x(x^{3}y^{5})(x^{2}y^{9})|=; x^{1} \u22c5 x^{3} \u22c5 y^{5} \u22c5 x^{2} \u22c5 y^{9}|=; (x^{1} \u22c5 x^{3} \u22c5 x^{2})(y^{5} \u22c5 y^{9})]</span></p><p>This way, we can apply the product rule to the two variables independently:\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: (x^{1} \u22c5 x^{3} \u22c5 x^{2})(y^{5} \u22c5 y^{9})|=; x^{(1 + 3 + 2)} \u22c5 y^{(5 + 9)}|=; x^{6}y^{14}]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "Here we have two variables, [MATH: x] and [MATH: y.] We will start by grouping the two variables separately:\n[MATH: x(x^{3}y^{5})(x^{2}y^{9})|=; x^{1} \u22c5 x^{3} \u22c5 y^{5} \u22c5 x^{2} \u22c5 y^{9}|=; (x^{1} \u22c5 x^{3} \u22c5 x^{2})(y^{5} \u22c5 y^{9})]\n\nThis way, we can apply the product rule to the two variables independently:\n[MATH: (x^{1} \u22c5 x^{3} \u22c5 x^{2})(y^{5} \u22c5 y^{9})|=; x^{(1 + 3 + 2)} \u22c5 y^{(5 + 9)}|=; x^{6}y^{14}]",
