        "readable_text": "Prompt:\nUse exponents to rewrite the product [MATH: b \u22c5 b^{2} \u22c5 c^{3} \u22c5 b^{4} \u22c5 c^{5} \u22c5 c^{6} \u22c5 b^{7}.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: b^{4} \u22c5 c^{3}]\nb. [MATH: b^{7} \u22c5 c^{6}]\nc. [MATH: b^{14} \u22c5 c^{14}]\nd. [MATH: b^{18} \u22c5 c^{11}]\ne. [MATH: b^{6} \u22c5 c^{7}]"
      }
    ],
    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nThe product rule for exponents states that when we multiply two or more exponents that have the same base, we add the exponents:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: a^n\\cdot a^m=a^{n+m}]\n\nContent:\nFor example,\n\nContent:\n[MATH: a^2\\cdot a^3 = a^{2+3} = a^5.]\n\nContent:\nTo see why the product rule is true, let's take the above example and work it out again, the long way.\n\nContent:\nFor the two factors [MATH: a^2] and [MATH: a^3] we have [MATH: a^2=a\\cdot a] and [MATH: a^3= a\\cdot a\\cdot a.] If we now substitute these two expressions into [MATH: a^2\\cdot a^3], we get the same result:\n[MATH: \\eqalign{\na^2\\cdot a^3&=\\\n(a\\cdot a) \\cdot (a\\cdot a\\cdot a)&=\\\na\\cdot a\\cdot a\\cdot a\\cdot a&=\\\na^5\n}]\n\nContent:\nThe product rule is just a shortcut for the process above.\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Using the Product Rule with Two Terms\n\nExample Prompt:\nSimplify [MATH: a^{9}\\cdot a^{7}.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe add the exponents, as follows:\n\n[MATH: a^{9} \u22c5 a^{7}|=; a^{9 + 7}|=; a^{16}]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\nSimplify [MATH: z^{17} \u22c5 z^{5}.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: z^{25}]\nb. [MATH: z^{19}]\nc. [MATH: z^{12}]\nd. [MATH: z^{22}]\ne. [MATH: z^{9}]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: x^{4} \u22c5 x^{3} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: x^{7}]\nb. [MATH: x]\nc. [MATH: x^{12}]\nd. [MATH: x^{5}]\ne. [MATH: x^{3}]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Using the Product Rule with Three or More Terms\n\nExample Prompt:\nFind [MATH: n] such that [MATH: x^2\\cdot x^3 \\cdot x^4 = x^n] is correct.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe add the exponents, as follows:\n\n[MATH: x^{2} \u22c5 x^{3} \u22c5 x^{4}|=; x^{2 + 3 + 4}|=; x^{9}]\n\nTherefore [MATH: x^{n}=x^9], so [MATH: n=9.]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\nFind [MATH: n] such that [MATH: z^{5} \u22c5 z^{7} \u22c5 z^{2} = z^{n}] is correct.\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 14]\nb. [MATH: 12]\nc. [MATH: 7]\nd. [MATH: 9]\ne. [MATH: 10]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\nFind [MATH: n] such that [MATH: y^{2} \u22c5 y^{3} \u22c5 y^{2} = y^{n}] is correct.\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 6]\nb. [MATH: 3]\nc. [MATH: 7]\nd. [MATH: 4]\ne. [MATH: 5]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Using the Product Rule with Multiple Variables\n\nExample Prompt:\nSimplify [MATH: x\\left(x^3 y^5\\right) \\left(x^2 y^9\\right).]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nHere we have two variables, [MATH: x] and [MATH: y.] We will start by grouping the two variables separately:\n[MATH: x(x^{3}y^{5})(x^{2}y^{9})|=; x^{1} \u22c5 x^{3} \u22c5 y^{5} \u22c5 x^{2} \u22c5 y^{9}|=; (x^{1} \u22c5 x^{3} \u22c5 x^{2})(y^{5} \u22c5 y^{9})]\n\nThis way, we can apply the product rule to the two variables independently:\n[MATH: (x^{1} \u22c5 x^{3} \u22c5 x^{2})(y^{5} \u22c5 y^{9})|=; x^{(1 + 3 + 2)} \u22c5 y^{(5 + 9)}|=; x^{6}y^{14}]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\nSimplify [MATH: (xz)(x^{2}y^{2})(x^{3}z^{3}).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: x^{6}y^{2}z^{3}]\nb. [MATH: x^{6}y^{2}z^{4}]\nc. [MATH: x^{3}yz^{2}]\nd. [MATH: x^{3}y^{3}z^{3}]\ne. [MATH: x^{3}y^{2}z^{3}]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\nUse exponents to rewrite the product [MATH: b \u22c5 b^{2} \u22c5 c^{3} \u22c5 b^{4} \u22c5 c^{5} \u22c5 c^{6} \u22c5 b^{7}.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: b^{4} \u22c5 c^{3}]\nb. [MATH: b^{7} \u22c5 c^{6}]\nc. [MATH: b^{14} \u22c5 c^{14}]\nd. [MATH: b^{18} \u22c5 c^{11}]\ne. [MATH: b^{6} \u22c5 c^{7}]"
